A menu that does not fit is moved, not lost.

NSPopUpWindow shifts its frame so the SELECTED item sits over the button, which is the whole point
of a popup button, and then set it without ever asking whether the result is on the screen. For a
button near the bottom of a window that puts the menu past the edge.

MEASURED ON A PATH NOBODY HAD DRIVEN. Swift Publisher Open Recent, with the button frame taken from
CIDER_TRACE_TREE rather than read off a capture:

  NSPopUpButton 160x25@20,11 win 160x25@20,11 top 648..673

so the click goes to 100,660 in window coordinates, and CIDER_WAYLAND_TRACE_INPUT confirms it
arrives there. The menu is then built, mapped and PAINTED:

  popup=ok number=3 size=72x38 level=6
  create=ok number=3 size=72x38 at=20,-27 level=6
  mapped=yes number=3 size=72x38
  pixels=drawn number=3 changed=2766/6600 colours=41
  present number=3 count=1 size=72x38

at=20,-27 is twenty seven points ABOVE the top of the output. The pointer then enters it and the
conversion to screen answers a negative y:

  pointer=enter x=80 y=34 window=3
  screenloc local=80,34 focusWindow=3 frame=Some((20.0, -18.0, 38.0)) -> 100,-14
  hide number=3 visible=true

The tracking loop reads that as outside the menu and hides it one event later. So the menu was
never missing: it was drawn, off screen, and then closed by its own tracking loop. Nothing in the
capture could have shown that, and three earlier attempts at this button failed on coordinates
before ever reaching it.

The clamp keeps the near edge when a menu is bigger than the screen, rather than pushing it off the
far one.

--- a/AppKit/NSPopUpWindow.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSPopUpWindow.m
@@ -18,6 +18,7 @@
